Props are values passed from a parent component to a child. function Parent() { return <Child name="Avhi" />; }

function Child({ name }) { return <h1>Hello {name}</h1>; } Props are read-only from the child's perspective. What is state? State is data managed by a component that can change over time. const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

When state changes, React schedules a re-render.

Component lifecycle For functional components, think of the lifecycle as: Mount -> Render -> Effects -> Update -> Render -> Effects -> Unmount

useEffect is commonly used for side effects and cleanup. useEffect(() => { const subscription = subscribe();

return () => { subscription.unsubscribe(); }; }, []); The cleanup runs when the component unmounts and before the effect runs again when dependencies change.

What is a custom hook? A custom hook is a JavaScript function starting with use that allows us to extract and reuse stateful React logic.

Example:

function useFetch(url) { const [data, setData] = useState(null); const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

useEffect(() => { fetch(url) .then(res => res.json()) .then(result => { setData(result); setLoading(false); }); }, [url]);

return { data, loading }; } Use it: function Users() { const { data, loading } = useFetch("/api/users");

// ... }

Important interview point

A custom hook shares logic, not state. If two components call: useFetch(...) each invocation has its own state unless the hook uses some shared external state mechanism.

How do you integrate APIs in React?

Example:

useEffect(() => { const fetchUsers = async () => { try { setLoading(true);

const response = await fetch("/api/users"); const data = await response.json();

setUsers(data); } catch (error) { setError(error.message); } finally { setLoading(false); } };

fetchUsers(); }, []); I normally handle: Loading Success Error Empty state Authentication Retry Caching REST methods GET -> Read POST -> Create PUT -> Replace PATCH -> Partial update DELETE -> Delete

